7. Why Do We Need Optimistic Locking?
Without it:
Transaction A reads value
        ↓
Transaction B reads same value
        ↓
A updates
        ↓
B updates using old information
        ↓
A's update may be overwritten ❌

With optimistic locking:
A reads Version 1
B reads Version 1

        ↓

A updates
Version → 2
        ↓
B tries Version 1
        ↓
VERSION MISMATCH
        ↓
Conflict detected ✅


Pessimistic Locking:
Definition: Pessimistic locking assumes that conflicts will occur, and it locks the data from the moment a transaction starts until it completes.

Real Life Example
Imagine
Public Toilet.
One person goes inside.
Locks door.
Nobody enters.
After finishing,
Door opens.
Exactly
Pessimistic Lock.

Which One is Faster?
Optimistic
Very Fast.
No waiting.

Pessimistic
Slower.
Users wait.

Which One is Safer?
Pessimistic.
Because only one user works at a time.

Performance Comparison
| Feature            | Optimistic         | Pessimistic                         |
| ------------------ | ------------------ | ----------------------------------- |
| Locks immediately? | ❌ No               | ✅ Yes                               |
| Waiting?           | No                 | Yes                                 |
| Performance        | Fast               | Slower                              |
| Conflict Handling  | At commit          | Before update                       |
| Best For           | Read-heavy systems | Write-heavy, conflict-prone systems |


